<%- include('../partials/header', { title: 'Integration Settings' }) %>

<div class="page-header">
  <h1>Integration Settings</h1>
</div>

<%- include('../partials/csvActions', { moduleKey: 'setting' }) %>

<% if (message) { %><div class="alert alert-success"><%= message %></div><% } %>
<% if (error) { %><div class="alert alert-error"><%= error %></div><% } %>

<div class="panel form-panel">
  <div class="panel-header"><h2>Slack &amp; Microsoft Teams Webhooks</h2></div>
  <p class="admin-tile-sub" style="margin-bottom:16px;">
    Sends notifications to Slack and/or Microsoft Teams via Incoming Webhooks. Nothing is hardcoded and no new
    account is created by this app — an Administrator generates each webhook URL from a channel your company
    already has, then pastes it in below. Leave either field blank to skip that channel entirely; nothing breaks
    if only one (or neither) is configured.
  </p>
  <form method="post" action="/admin/integrations">
    <div class="form-grid">
      <label>Slack Webhook URL
        <input type="url" name="slackWebhookUrl" value="<%= slackWebhookUrl %>" placeholder="https://hooks.slack.com/services/..." />
      </label>
      <label>Microsoft Teams Webhook URL
        <input type="url" name="teamsWebhookUrl" value="<%= teamsWebhookUrl %>" placeholder="https://outlook.office.com/webhook/..." />
      </label>
    </div>
    <div class="form-actions">
      <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">Save Settings</button>
    </div>
  </form>
  <details style="margin-top:16px;">
    <summary>How do I get a webhook URL?</summary>
    <p><strong>Slack:</strong> Apps → Incoming Webhooks → Add to Slack → choose a channel → copy the Webhook URL.</p>
    <p><strong>Microsoft Teams:</strong> open the channel → ⋯ → Connectors → Incoming Webhook → configure → copy the Webhook URL.</p>
  </details>
</div>

<div class="panel form-panel">
  <div class="panel-header"><h2>Integration API</h2></div>
  <p class="admin-tile-sub" style="margin-bottom:16px;">
    A token-gated REST API so an outside system — a monitoring tool, a company website's contact form, a CI
    pipeline — can file or read tickets without a human logging in. Nothing is sent anywhere on its own: it only
    answers requests that arrive with the key below in an <code>X-Api-Key</code> header. Disabled (every request
    gets a 503) until you generate a key.
  </p>
  <% if (apiToken) { %>
    <div class="form-grid">
      <label>Current API Key
        <input type="text" readonly value="<%= apiToken %>" onclick="this.select()" style="font-family: monospace;" />
      </label>
    </div>
    <div class="form-actions">
      <form method="post" action="/admin/integrations/api-key/generate" style="display:inline;">
        <button type="submit" class="btn btn-secondary">Regenerate (invalidates the key above)</button>
      </form>
      <form method="post" action="/admin/integrations/api-key/revoke" style="display:inline;" onsubmit="return confirm('Disable the Integration API? Every /api/v1/* request will start failing until a new key is generated.');">
        <button type="submit" class="btn btn-danger">Revoke</button>
      </form>
    </div>
  <% } else { %>
    <div class="form-actions">
      <form method="post" action="/admin/integrations/api-key/generate">
        <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">Generate API Key</button>
      </form>
    </div>
  <% } %>
  <details style="margin-top:16px;" <%= apiToken ? 'open' : '' %>>
    <summary>API reference</summary>
    <p>Send the key in an <code>X-Api-Key</code> header on every request. All bodies/responses are JSON.</p>
    <table class="table" style="margin-top:8px;">
      <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Path</th><th>Body / notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>POST</td><td>/api/v1/incidents</td><td>employeeName, department, location, category, priority, subject, description</td></tr>
        <tr><td>GET</td><td>/api/v1/incidents</td><td>optional ?status=&priority=&limit= (max 200)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>GET</td><td>/api/v1/incidents/:incidentId</td><td>lookup by Incident ID, e.g. INC-2026-000123</td></tr>
        <tr><td>POST</td><td>/api/v1/requests</td><td>requester, department, catalogItem, details</td></tr>
        <tr><td>POST</td><td>/api/v1/problems</td><td>title, description (owner, linkedIncidents optional)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>POST</td><td>/api/v1/changes</td><td>title, description, riskLevel, plannedDate, requestedBy, department</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:8px;">Example:</p>
    <pre style="white-space:pre-wrap;">curl -X POST https://YOUR-APP.onrender.com/api/v1/incidents \
  -H "X-Api-Key: YOUR_KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"employeeName":"Jane Doe","department":"IT","location":"HQ","category":"Network","priority":"High","subject":"VPN down","description":"Cannot connect to VPN since 9am."}'</pre>
    <p>Every ticket created this way runs through the same automation rules, SLA calculation, and audit trail as one filed in the browser — it just shows "via Integration API" in its audit entry.</p>
  </details>
</div>

<div class="panel">
  <div class="panel-header"><h2>Test</h2></div>
  <p class="admin-tile-sub" style="margin-bottom:16px;">Sends a one-off test message to every channel that's configured above.</p>
  <form method="post" action="/admin/integrations/test">
    <button type="submit" class="btn btn-secondary">Send Test Notification</button>
  </form>
</div>

<div class="panel">
  <div class="panel-header"><h2>Expiry Digest</h2></div>
  <p class="admin-tile-sub" style="margin-bottom:16px;">
    Posts a one-time digest of everything expiring soon — contracts, asset warranties, vendor AMCs, and software
    licenses (the same items on the Reports page) — to Slack/Teams. The original tool emailed this to HR
    automatically every morning; the free hosting this app runs on has no scheduler, so this is sent on demand
    instead.
  </p>
  <form method="post" action="/admin/integrations/send-digest">
    <button type="submit" class="btn btn-secondary">Send Expiry Digest Now</button>
  </form>
</div>

<div class="panel">
  <div class="panel-header"><h2>SLA Breach Alerts</h2></div>
  <p class="admin-tile-sub" style="margin-bottom:16px;">
    Every open incident already shows a live SLA badge (Breached/At Risk/On Track) on the Incident list and its
    own page. This posts a Slack/Teams alert for any incident that has breached its SLA since the last time this
    was run — same on-demand pattern as the Expiry Digest above, for the same reason (no free-tier scheduler to
    run it automatically). Safe to click as often as you like: an incident already flagged is never alerted twice
    unless its status changes.
  </p>
  <form method="post" action="/admin/integrations/check-sla-breaches">
    <button type="submit" class="btn btn-secondary">Check SLA Breaches Now</button>
  </form>
</div>

<%- include('../partials/footer') %>
